{"uid":"1a366d5ea6cbfeb9","name":"test_a_crash_between_the_counter_persist_and_the_unconsume_never_exceeds_the_cap","fullName":"tests.test_steer#test_a_crash_between_the_counter_persist_and_the_unconsume_never_exceeds_the_cap","historyId":"4c1f6fa8d6c94ca6355e5f40261ea2e3","time":{"start":1791976638869,"stop":1791976638902,"duration":33},"description":"PRD requirement D.5 (#184): `_handle_unsettled_steering` writes\n    `steerRedeliveries` and `STEER_DEFERRED_REDELIVERY_STATUS_KEY` in the\n    ONE atomic `update_status` call at the tail of that method -- strictly\n    BEFORE `_flush_deferred_steering_redelivery` (called from a later,\n    separate, non-steer iteration) ever performs the actual\n    `unconsume_steering` write. Before task 014 (whose restructuring this\n    test pins), `_handle_unsettled_steering` called `unconsume_steering`\n    immediately, per name, INSIDE its own loop, and only persisted the\n    counter in a single `update_status` call AFTER that whole loop had\n    finished -- so a crash landing between those two writes left the\n    message visibly redelivered (pending again) while the counter on disk\n    still read the OLD value, letting the message exceed its cap across\n    enough crash/resume cycles.\n\n    This test kills the engine -- a brand-new `LoopSupervisor` over the\n    same run root, carrying no in-memory state, the same resume double\n    every other test in this module already uses -- exactly inside that\n    window: right after `_handle_unsettled_steering`'s counter/deferred\n    write has landed on disk, but before `_flush_deferred_steering_\n    redelivery` has ever run for this message. The cap must still hold\n    once the resumed process keeps going.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>PRD requirement D.5 (#184): <code>_handle_unsettled_steering</code> writes<br />\n<code>steerRedeliveries</code> and <code>STEER_DEFERRED_REDELIVERY_STATUS_KEY</code> in the<br />\nONE atomic <code>update_status</code> call at the tail of that method -- strictly<br />\nBEFORE <code>_flush_deferred_steering_redelivery</code> (called from a later,<br />\nseparate, non-steer iteration) ever performs the actual<br />\n<code>unconsume_steering</code> write. Before task 014 (whose restructuring this<br />\ntest pins), <code>_handle_unsettled_steering</code> called <code>unconsume_steering</code><br />\nimmediately, per name, INSIDE its own loop, and only persisted the<br />\ncounter in a single <code>update_status</code> call AFTER that whole loop had<br />\nfinished -- so a crash landing between those two writes left the<br />\nmessage visibly redelivered (pending again) while the counter on disk<br />\nstill read the OLD value, letting the message exceed its cap across<br />\nenough crash/resume cycles.</p>\n<pre><code>This test kills the engine -- a brand-new `LoopSupervisor` over the\nsame run root, carrying no in-memory state, the same resume double\nevery other test in this module already uses -- exactly inside that\nwindow: right after `_handle_unsettled_steering`'s counter/deferred\nwrite has landed on disk, but before `_flush_deferred_steering_\nredelivery` has ever run for this message. The cap must still hold\nonce the resumed process keeps going.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791976554785,"stop":1791976554785,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791976554786,"stop":1791976554786,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"_isolate_ambient_home_in_process","time":{"start":1791976638859,"stop":